---
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";
import ProjectGallery from "../../components/ProjectGallery.astro";

const project = projects.find(
    p => p.slug === "end-suction-water-pump"
);

if (!project) {
    throw new Error("Project not found");
}
---

<Layout>

<div class="project-page">

    <a class="back-link" href="/projects">
        ← Back to Projects
    </a>

<section class="project-header">

    <div class="project-summary">

        <img
            src={project.companyLogo}
            alt={`${project.company} logo`}
            class={`project-company-logo ${project.companyLogoClass}`}
        />

        <h1>{project.title}</h1>


    </div>

    <div class={`project-hero-image ${project.heroImageClass}`}>

        <img
            src={project.heroImage}
            alt={project.title}
            class="project-hero"
        />

    </div>

</section>

<hr class="experience-divider" />

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Overview
    </h3>

    <p>
        The project focused on the SIHI ZLND Overhung Industrial Process
        Pump and was initiated as a follow-up to a product portfolio
        rationalisation initiative. Its objective was to further reduce 
        manufacturing cost and complexity by standardising components across 
        the remaining pump product lines and increasing interchangeability
        between different configurations.
    </p>

    <p>
        I joined the project during the redesign phase to support the 
        engineering team with mechanical design and CAD development. My 
        responsibility was to redesign the driven-end assembly of the long-
        coupled pump configuration, separating the originally integrated 
        bearing housing and cover into two independent components.
    </p>

</section>

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Project Development
    </h3>

    <p>
        The project aimed to establish a common volute casing design for close-coupled
        and long-coupled pump configurations. While both shared the
        same pump casing, their mechanical interfaces differed.
    </p>

    <p>
        In close-coupled configurations, the casing was connected directly to
        the motor through an adapter, with the impeller mounted on the motor 
        shaft or a shaft extension. In long-coupled configurations, the casing
        connected to a separate bearing housing through a cover. The bearing
        housing supported the pump shaft, which connected to the motor shaft.
    </p>

        <h4>Driven-End Redesign</h4>

    <p>
        For the long-coupled configuration, I redesigned the original integrated
        bearing housing and cover as separate components. The new
        architecture was based on two standard bearing housing sizes, with 
        interchangeable covers providing the connection to different pump
        casings.
    </p>

    <p>
        I developed the new components in Solidworks, using the original design 
        as a starting point where applicable. This involved creating a new 
        mechanical interface between the bearing housing and cover, as well as 
        incorporating additional design features. These included a machined 
        surface on top of the bearing housing for a standardised nameplate.
    </p>

    <p>
        I also introduced a cast Flowserve logo on the sides of the bearing
        housing as a design detail. Although not part of the original 
        requirements, the project team welcomed the addition and retained it in
        the final design.
    </p>

        <h4>International Collaboration</h4>

    <p>
        The project brought together engineers from several Flowserve
        locations, including Spain, Germany and India, who were responsible for
        different aspects of the product development. I coordinated with the 
        other engineers throughout the redesign phase to ensure that the
        components and interfaces developed in parallel remained aligned.
    </p>

</section>

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Outcome
    </h3>

    <p>
        The redesigned components contributed to a more standardised pump
        product range, reducing the number of unique parts and overall product 
        complexity. The resulting component commonality across different 
        pump configurations also contributed to manufacturing cost savings.
    </p>

    <p>
        My contribution focused on the mechanical design and CAD
        development of the long-coupled driven-end assembly, supporting the
        wider team's modularisation initiative.
    </p>

</section>

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        Gallery
    </h3>

    <ProjectGallery
        images={[
            {
                src: "/images/projects/end-suction-water-pump/gallery-1.jpg",
                alt: "SIHI ZLND overhung industrial process pump shown in blue.",
                caption: "Original design"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/end-suction-water-pump/gallery-2.jpg",
                alt: "SIHI ZLND pump assembly.",
                caption: "Redesigned pump"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/end-suction-water-pump/gallery-3.jpg",
                alt: "Cross-sectional view of SIHI ZLND pump assembly showing the internal components, shaft and bearing arrangement.",
                caption: "Redesigned pump section-view"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/end-suction-water-pump/gallery-4.jpg",
                alt: "Prototype assembly during engineering development.",
                caption: "Prototype"
            },
            {
                src: "/images/projects/end-suction-water-pump/gallery-5.jpg",
                alt: "Close-up of the redesigned protoype bearing housing.",
                caption: "Prototype"
            }
        ]}
    />

</section>


{project.references && (

<section>

    <h3 class="company-section-title">
        References
    </h3>

    <div class="project-references">

        {project.references.map(reference => (

            <a
                href={reference.url}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
            >
                {reference.text} →
            </a>

        ))}

    </div>

</section>

)}


</div>

</Layout>